WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.410
It seems that Emperor Palpatine owes his epic
Star Wars return to another guy's exit — that's

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:10.530
according to a new interview with Colin Trevorrow
in Empire — the magazine, not the pan-galactic

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:11.660
evil organization.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:15.320
During this interview, Trevorrow, who was
at one point attached to write and direct

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:19.920
the ninth film in the Skywalker Saga, revealed
that it was J.J. Abrams who decided to bring

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.380
back the iconic character.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.680
“Haha.”

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:30.160
Trevorrow's remarks to Empire focused on his
time working on Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker,

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.800
which he and his writing partner Derek Connolly
departed in September 2017.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:39.620
The director, who is best known as the driving
creative force behind the Jurassic World franchise,

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:44.390
told the magazine that the return of the notorious
Sith Lord was the story element that finally

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.570
helped the rest of the film's plot fall into
place.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:47.240
He explained:

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.800
"Bringing back the Emperor was an idea J.J.
brought to the table when he came on board.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.520
It's honestly something I never considered.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:54.880
I commend him for it.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.720
This was a tough story to unlock, and he found
the key."

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:02.460
Trevorrow spent enough time working on The
Rise of Skywalker to score a story credit,

00:01:02.469 --> 00:01:06.479
but he wasn't willing to go into detail when
asked about his specific contributions to

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:06.980
the script.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:07.920
He told Empire:

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.380
"That's not my place, especially right now.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:14.600
I know how hard this crew has worked over
the past two years, a lot of them are working

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.860
with us now on [the next Jurassic World movie].

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.880
I want to support them as they put this film
out into the world.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.400
In the end, I'm just proud to have my name
in space."

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.060
He did, however, give credit to Abrams for
keeping what worked and jettisoning what didn't

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.200
in regard to The Rise of Skywalker's story.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.400
Trevorrow explained:

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.020
"I'm not sure anyone who chooses the creative
life should expect things.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.920
But I'm grateful to JJ for embracing some
of our ideas.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.180
It's exciting that fans will get to see the
moments that felt essential to all of us."

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:46.540
Abrams, of course, co-wrote and directed 2015's
The Force Awakens and in a recent conversation

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:51.069
with Uproxx, the director revealed that the
seeds for Emperor Palpatine's eventual return

00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:54.600
had been planted all the way back when that
film was still in the scripting stage.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:55.380
Abrams said:

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.140
"When you look at this as nine chapters of
a story, perhaps the weirder thing would be

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:00.820
if Palpatine didn't return.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.940
[...] Strangely, his absence entirely from
the third trilogy would be conspicuous.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.060
[...] When [co-writer Lawrence] Kasdan and
I worked on The Force Awakens, we didn't do

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:09.600
it in a vacuum.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.480
We very purposely looked at what came before
[...] to ask, where does this feel like it's

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:13.980
going?"

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:19.540
Fortunately, The Last Jedi director Rian Johnson
didn't step on Abrams' toes in crafting that

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:20.040
film's story.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.000
Abrams remembered:

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.260
"When Rian was brought on, we met and we talked
about things, and he wrote his story.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.240
And when I read the script, I realized this
didn't get in the way of anything [Kasdan]

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:30.300
and I talked about."

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:34.500
As it turns out, that particular stroke of
luck has been significant to the production

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.400
of The Last Jedi's upcoming sequel.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.540
Abrams continued:

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.740
"There were some very specific things we did
get to do in [Rise of Skywalker] that we were

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:46.000
laughing and going, 'Oh my god, we're finally
doing that thing we talked about five years

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:46.500
ago.'"

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.980
Of course, Palpatine's role in the movie is
still a total mystery, so nobody can be sure

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:55.650
exactly what Abrams and Kasdan talked about
a half decade ago that will finally come to

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:56.200
fruition.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:00.820
Did the Emperor survive being tossed into
a seemingly bottomless abyss by Anakin Skywalker

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:02.740
at the conclusion of Return of the Jedi?

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:07.090
Or was he resurrected through some arcane
means, possibly by harnessing the mysterious

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:09.040
power of the Dark Side of the Force?

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:13.420
Above all else, what on earth could his master
plan possibly be, and how does it involve

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:17.640
the burgeoning Jedi Rey and Kylo Ren, heir
apparent to the Empire's legacy?

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.000
Only time will tell.

